VulkanOptics (optics::)
======================

The compositing pass has two disjoint paths:
- Glass: fast screen-space refraction sampling a linearly filtered PCloud
  HDR image, with Snell-derived bounded distortion, Fresnel and mild
  thickness-dependent tint attenuation. No per-pixel BVH, no direct-light
  highlight injection, no temporal filter.
- Mirrors: full deterministic Whitted-style BVH reflections, using PCloud
  probes for indirect lighting at offscreen hits.

The host may supply an empty surface scene for glass-only compositions;
the optics compositor then has no reflection BVH to build. In scenes
containing mirrors, the shared BVH includes all scene geometry so mirrors
can continue to reflect off-camera objects. Glass and mirror surfaces are
resolved in separate fullscreen-scissor draws, each discarding pixels
that do not belong to its material.

Glass is a performance-oriented approximation: it is NOT complete
path-traced volume refraction. Its bounded screen-space distortion avoids
the strong inversion and very high GPU costs of full-resolution
multi-bounce sphere tracing. Missing offscreen background data and
accurate caustics remain limitations. Thin glass keeps zero displacement,
while solid glass has controlled distortion. Point-light glints are not
injected; mirror quality is unchanged.

BVH topology uses VulkanBVH stackless, per-swapchain skinned refits,
PCloud probes and host-owned G-buffers. The standalone VulkanPMap is
not linked into VulkanOptics.
